WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.317
PQC migration starts with knowing where cryptography is actually used.

00:00:04.317 --> 00:00:09.377
That sounds simple until an organization tries to enumerate certificates, TLS endpoints,

00:00:09.377 --> 00:00:13.593
VPN profiles, SSH keys, API clients, code-signing systems, firmware-signing processes,

00:00:13.593 --> 00:00:17.388
package repositories, database encryption, backups, embedded devices, cloud services,

00:00:17.388 --> 00:00:20.340
third-party products, cryptographic libraries, hardware security modules,

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.026
and key management platforms.

00:00:22.026 --> 00:00:25.821
Cryptography often hides inside products, dependencies, and default configurations.

00:00:25.821 --> 00:00:29.616
A useful inventory records more than an algorithm name.

00:00:29.616 --> 00:00:34.676
It should capture the business owner, technical owner, system purpose, cryptographic role,

00:00:34.676 --> 00:00:38.893
library or product dependency, key location, certificate authority, data sensitivity,

00:00:38.893 --> 00:00:42.688
data retention requirement, external exposure, operational criticality, vendor involvement,

00:00:42.688 --> 00:00:44.374
and current migration status.

00:00:44.374 --> 00:00:46.904
Without that context, teams cannot prioritize.

00:00:46.904 --> 00:00:51.964
A test system with no sensitive data does not carry the same

00:00:51.964 --> 00:00:55.759
urgency as an internet-facing service protecting long-lived regulated data.

00:00:55.759 --> 00:01:00.397
Discovery is difficult because the same organization may have centralized platforms,

00:01:00.397 --> 00:01:03.349
independently managed applications, old appliances, unmanaged certificates,

00:01:03.349 --> 00:01:08.831
developer-created integrations, cloud services, and legacy systems that no one wants to touch.

00:01:08.831 --> 00:01:11.782
Some cryptography is visible in network scans.

00:01:11.782 --> 00:01:13.891
Some appears in configuration management.

00:01:13.891 --> 00:01:18.951
Some lives in CI/CD pipelines, mobile apps, update channels, or vendor-managed services.

00:01:18.951 --> 00:01:24.432
Some requires interviews with teams that understand how a system is really operated.

00:01:24.432 --> 00:01:28.649
The goal is not a perfect spreadsheet on day one.

00:01:28.649 --> 00:01:34.552
The goal is a living cryptographic inventory that improves over time and supports decisions.

00:01:34.552 --> 00:01:37.925
Start with exposed services and long-lived confidential data,

00:01:37.925 --> 00:01:41.720
then expand into internal systems, software supply chain functions,

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:42.985
and third-party dependencies.

00:01:42.985 --> 00:01:48.467
Good inventory work turns PQC from an abstract concern into a concrete roadmap:

00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:53.527
here are the systems, here are the roles, here are the blockers,

00:01:53.527 --> 00:01:56.900
and here is what can be tested next.
